# d4-m1 — Workflow Integration and Solution Design, multiple-response
# Targets official objectives 4.2 (research, planning, process optimisation) and
# 4.3 (solution design, development, iteration), both previously thin.
research-and-planning|2|2|An associate wants to use Claude to help plan a project rather than to execute its tasks.|Ask it to surface options and their trade-offs|Keep the decision about which option to take itself|Ask it to choose the plan and then follow that plan|Ask for a single recommended plan with no alternatives|Use it only once the plan has already been decided|Planning assistance works by widening the options and making the trade-offs visible, while the choice between them stays a human judgement.|Delegating the choice moves a decision that needs accountability, a single recommendation hides the alternatives, and waiting until after forfeits the help.
research-and-planning|3|2|A team uses Claude for background research before scoping a piece of work.|Ask for sources that can be checked independently|Treat the output as a starting map, not a conclusion|Treat the summary as settled once it reads coherently|Ask for the most comprehensive possible overview|Skip verification, since research output cites sources|Research assistance is valuable for orientation, and its claims still need traceable sources because coherence is not evidence.|Comprehensiveness without a question produces volume, and citations are a starting point for checking rather than a substitute for it.
research-and-planning|4|2|A team asks Claude to optimise a process and gets a plausible redesign they cannot evaluate.|Check the redesign against how the process actually runs|Establish the current baseline before adopting any change|Adopt it, since the reasoning behind it is coherent|Ask for a second redesign and compare the two versions|Adopt it partially and see whether things improve|A proposed optimisation can only be judged against the real process and a measured baseline, without which improvement cannot even be detected.|Coherence is not fit, a second proposal compares two ungrounded designs, and partial adoption changes the process while still unable to measure it.
solution-design|2|2|An associate is using Claude to help design a solution and wants the iteration to be productive.|State the constraints the solution has to satisfy|Revise the design as the constraints become clearer|Ask for a finished design in a single request|Accept the first design, since iterating wastes time|Change several parts at once between each revision|Design work is iterative against constraints, so naming them and revising as they sharpen is what makes each round add something.|One-shot design skips the discovery, refusing to iterate forfeits it, and changing several things at once hides which change helped.
solution-design|3|2|A team is using Claude through several rounds of solution development and losing track of decisions.|Record the decisions separately from the conversation|Restate the settled decisions when starting a new round|Rely on the conversation to hold the earlier decisions|Ask the model to summarise what has been decided so far|Start over whenever the thread becomes hard to follow|Decisions that matter need to live outside the conversation, because a long thread loses its early content and a generated summary inherits that loss.|Relying on the thread is the failure, a self-summary cannot recover what is out of reach, and starting over discards the work.
solution-design|4|2|A team must decide how much of a solution to design with Claude before building anything.|Enough to expose the decisions that are expensive to reverse|Stop where further design stops reducing uncertainty|Design the whole solution in full before any building|Build immediately and let the design emerge as you go|Design until the model stops offering new suggestions|Design earns its cost by reducing uncertainty about irreversible choices, which is what sets both how far to go and when to stop.|Full up-front design over-invests, building with none leaves expensive choices unexamined, and exhausting the model's suggestions is not a stopping rule.
automation-boundaries|2|2|A team is deciding which step of a process to automate first.|A step whose output is already checked downstream|A step whose rules can actually be written down|The step that takes people the longest to perform|The step the team finds the most tedious to do|The step that the fewest people are involved in|The first automation should be one you can specify and one where being wrong is caught anyway, which makes it cheap to learn on.|Duration, tedium and headcount describe the cost of doing the step rather than the cost of getting it wrong.
automation-boundaries|3|2|A step could be automated and any mistake would blend invisibly into correct-looking output.|Automate it only with a way to detect the silent errors|Treat detectability as the condition, not the accuracy|Automate it, since 99% beats what people achieve here|Leave it manual, since silent errors are unacceptable|Automate it and review a sample of outputs each month|A silent error reaches its destination unchallenged, so detection is what makes an accuracy figure safe to rely on.|Comparing to human accuracy ignores that human errors are noisier, a blanket refusal forgoes a strong step, and monthly sampling may never catch a rare silent failure.
automation-boundaries|4|2|A team automates a step and the person who did it now spends the same time checking it.|Compare what the checking costs against the old step|Ask whether the work moved rather than reduced|Accept it, since a person is still involved in the step|Automate the checking as well to free the person|Remove the check, since the automation is working|If checking costs what doing cost, the automation relocated the work instead of reducing it, and only the comparison shows which happened.|Human involvement is not the goal, automating a check produces something else to check, and removing it before knowing its value is premature.
human-in-the-loop|2|2|A team is deciding where to place the single human checkpoint in a workflow.|Immediately before the output has any real effect|Where the reviewer can still change the outcome|At the very end, after every step has finished running|At whichever step the team has capacity to staff|At the start, so the inputs are checked first|A checkpoint exists to stop a bad output doing damage, so it sits at the last moment before effect and only counts if the reviewer can act.|The end may already be past the point of effect, capacity determines staffing rather than placement, and input checks catch a different class of problem.
human-in-the-loop|3|2|A checkpoint has rejected two items in a year and the team is debating whether to keep it.|Look at what has escaped the review step so far|Find out whether the rejections would have mattered|Trust it, since a high approval rate suggests quality|Add a second reviewer to increase the scrutiny|Remove the step, since it rejects almost nothing|A checkpoint's value is what it prevents, so the escapes and the caught cases are the evidence rather than the approval rate.|A high rate is consistent with a good process and a useless one, adding reviewers changes it before diagnosing it, and removing it discards a possibly working control.
human-in-the-loop|4|2|A sign-off sits with a manager who does not read the technical content they are signing.|Move the approval to someone able to evaluate it|An approval without competence is a signature, not a control|Train the current approver over the coming months|Give the approver a checklist to work through instead|Remove the step, since the approval means nothing|Oversight requires the capacity to judge, so the fix is to put the approval where that judgement already exists.|Training is slower than reassignment, a checklist substitutes procedure for judgement, and removing it loses a control that should exist somewhere.
failure-modes|2|2|A team is asked what their workflow should do with an input it cannot handle.|Stop and tell a person, rather than produce a result|Make the failure visible rather than a silent one|Produce the best result it can from what it received|Skip that input and continue with the remaining ones|Retry the input until it eventually goes through|An input with no correct output should halt visibly, because a plausible answer to an unhandleable input is the most expensive failure available.|A best effort produces exactly that failure, silent skipping loses work unnoticed, and retrying a structurally bad input never succeeds.
failure-modes|3|2|A workflow has a fallback branch that no input has ever triggered since it was written.|Test it deliberately, since it is untested behaviour|Recognise that time elapsed is not evidence it works|Remove it, since eighteen months suggests it is unused|Assume it will work when it is eventually needed|Rewrite it to cover a wider range of failures|Untested behaviour stays untested however long it sits there, and the first real failure is the worst moment to discover it does not work.|Absence of firing is not evidence of redundancy, assuming it works is the risk, and widening scope adds more untested behaviour.
failure-modes|4|2|A team must choose between a workflow that fails loudly and stops, and one that continues degraded.|Compare what a wrong result costs against a stopped process|Let the process's own tolerance decide between them|Always fail loudly, since silent degradation is dangerous|Always continue, since stopping disrupts everyone downstream|Fail loudly in testing and continue quietly in production|Both behaviours are defensible and the right one depends on what the surrounding process can absorb, which makes the cost comparison the decision.|Absolute rules ignore the process, and differing between test and production is exactly where the untested path lives.
handoffs|2|2|A workflow passes output to a colleague who has no context for what was asked.|Pass the original request along with the output|Give the receiver what they need to judge the work|Ask the colleague to request context when they need it|Add a note describing the output's general purpose|Have the colleague run the workflow themselves instead|A handoff works when the receiver can evaluate what they have been given, and the request is what makes the output interpretable.|On-demand context adds a round trip every time, a general note is not the specific request, and moving the work is heavier than the gap requires.
handoffs|3|2|One team changes its output layout and the team downstream finds their step has broken.|Write the interface down as an agreement between them|Treat it as a shared commitment rather than one side's choice|Let the sending team decide, since they produce it|Let the receiving team decide, since they consume it|Support both formats so neither team has to change|An interface between two teams is a joint commitment, and writing it down is what stops it drifting whenever either side changes.|Unilateral decisions recreate the dispute, and supporting both doubles the surface that has to keep working.
measuring-value|2|2|A team is asked at a review whether the effort they put into a workflow has paid off.|Compare the outcome it targets before and after|Include the effort spent building and maintaining it|Report how many items the workflow has processed|Report how many people on the team now use it|Report how much time the team believes it saves|Value is a change in the targeted outcome set against the full cost, which is what makes the case rather than describing activity.|Volume measures activity, adoption measures uptake, and a believed saving is an impression rather than a measurement.
measuring-value|3|2|A trial with three volunteers looked transformative and the department-wide numbers are flat.|Look at how the pilot group differed from everyone else|Expect self-selected participants to outperform|Conclude the pilot must have been measured incorrectly|Roll back, since the benefit did not reproduce|Extend the rollout period until the benefit appears|Pilots run with motivated people on selected work, so the difference between that group and the rest is usually the whole explanation.|Measurement error is one hypothesis among several, rolling back discards the question, and more time does not change who is using it.
measuring-value|4|2|A change made the finance team faster and the operations team slower by about the same.|Measure the process end to end rather than per team|Ask whether cost moved rather than actually reduced|Report the improvement, since it is real for that team|Report both sets of numbers and leave it there|Reverse the change, since it clearly made things worse|A gain beside a loss elsewhere is usually cost relocating, and only an end-to-end view distinguishes that from value created.|Reporting one side is the error, reporting both without combining leaves the question open, and reversing assumes the answer.
repeatability|3|2|An auditor may ask a team to reproduce a quarterly figure long after it was produced.|Record the prompt, the inputs and the settings used|Recognise the output as the thing being reproduced|Record the output so it can be compared later on|Ask the same person to run it again next year|Note the date on which it was most recently run|Reproducibility means capturing everything that determined the run, because the output is the result rather than the recipe.|The same person cannot recall the settings, and a date records when rather than how.
team-adoption|4|2|Staff using a new workflow say it has taken the interesting part of their work away.|Take the concern seriously as a real design input|Recognise that how it lands decides whether it survives|Explain that the workflow frees them for better work|Proceed anyway, since the improvement is objectively real|Pause indefinitely until people feel differently about it|Adoption depends on the people doing the work, which makes their experience part of the design rather than an objection to be managed.|Explaining talks past the concern, proceeding on merit is how good tools get abandoned, and an indefinite pause waits for a feeling to change unaided.
